No, you're right--constraints on the latent means are not part of invariance testing--that's more like an analog of a t-test or one-way ANOVA. For invariance testing, typically one mean is fixed to zero for reference, and any others are freely estimated, though you can also fix the reference through indicator intercepts if you'd rather.

