> xz<-riskc*PSc
>
> mod1<-lm(MH~ riskc+PSc+xz+gender+age)

This came up in an earlier thread, where I explained that you need to be consistent in where you store your new variables.  If you use the data= argument to tell lm() where to find variables, then each variable should be in that data= set.  The syntax above does not provide a data= argument to lm(), so although it can find the objects riskc and xz, it would not find PSc, which is stored in semdata.  However, you calculated xz from an object called PSc, so clearly you created that stand-alone object outside the syntax you shared.

Regardless...

> summary(mod1, fit.measures=TRUE)

mod1 is an lm object, not a lavaan object.

> In order to test the unique contribution of PS as a moderator, I wanted to compare the main model of interest with two other models including each potential moderator using the model chi square, the CFI and RMSEA to evaluate goodness-of-fit.

In order for models to be comparable, they need to be fit to the same variables (including interaction terms).  So you would need to include all moderators in each model, and take turns fixing effects of different moderators to zero.  Then models can be compared, but only using fit measures that do not require nesting, such as information criteria (e.g., AIC, BIC) and incremental fit indices (e.g., CFI, TLI).

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

