Message-ID: <yYwRGecY7eQ.1wS8_mu7AQAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <yYwRGecY7eQ.x-etoureAAAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <yYwRGecY7eQ.x-etoureAAAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/yYwRGecY7eQ/m/1wS8_mu7AQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Regression" factor scores (and "Bartlett" factor scores) do NOT
preserve the correlation structure among the latent variables.

For a discussion, see

Berge, J., Krijnen, W., Wansbeek, T., & Shapiro, A. (1999). SOME NEW
RESULTS ON CORRELATION-PRESERVING FACTOR SCORES PREDICTION METHODS.
Linear Algebra and its Applications, 289, 311-318.

Yves.

On 8/27/20 8:58 PM, alwill...@gmail.com wrote:
> Hello,
>
> When trying to estimate factor scores for an orthogonal model using
> lavaan, I noticed that the factor scores end up being substantially
> correlated anyway (though correlations are higher in magnitude if the
> model is not orthogonal).  I'm wondering whether this behavior is
> expected or I've made some mistake in how I'm estimating factor scores.
> I noticed the same pattern with plausible values as well.  The below
> code will demonstrate these correlations.  Any clarity would be greatly
> appreciated.  Thank you!
>
>
> library(lavaan)
> library(psych)
>
> ## fit model
> HS.model <- ' visual =~ x1 + x2 + x3
> textual =~ x4 + x5 + x6
> speed =~ x7 + x8 + x9 '
> fit <- cfa(HS.model, data = HolzingerSwineford1939, orthogonal = TRUE)
>
> ### in R
> fs <- lavPredict(fit, method = "regression")
>
> ### R plausible values
> pv <- plausibleValues(fit)
> pv.1 <- pv[[2]]
> colnames(pv.1) <- c("ID.pv", "visual.pv", "textual.pv", "speed.pv")
> pv.1 <- pv.1[, 2:4]
>
> ### bind all the columns
> total <- cbind(fs, pv.1)
> # correlations among variables
> cor.plot(total, upper = FALSE)
>
>

